{"id": "0a7257c4-55ae-4ef2-960e-29786eeefce5", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T13:57:44.140226", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "b191063c-233b-4eaf-b328-059ab57cb461", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "I have a clear picture of the environment. Let me do a couple more quick checks (Python 3.14 vs JAX compatibility documentation is external knowledge; I'll confirm CPU/threads and write a small probe of what a measurement loop would look like), then compose the plan.\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "Key environment facts confirmed:\n- No numpy, scipy, jax, jaxlib, gymnasium, pettingzoo, torch — nothing. This is a bare \"system Python 3.14.4\".\n- Network disabled — cannot pip install.\n- 224 CPUs, 2 TB RAM, x86_64.\n\nSo this environment cannot execute ANY part of the benchmark (neither stack), and no repo source is present. The design must:\n1. Be honest that the repos/inputs are not present in /workspace (only task.json), so the design is grounded in the canonical upstream layouts of JaxMARL and PettingZoo MPE (which I know well), and pins versions to fetch at implementation time.\n2. Specify a target execution environment (separate from this sandbox) e.g., Python 3.11/3.12 venv on same hardware, with pinned deps.\n\nLet me now recall the canonical repo structure precisely to cite file paths/function names. I'll be careful to only assert what I'm confident about, and flag uncertainty where appropriate.\n\nJaxMARL (github.com/FLAIROx/JaxMARL), version ~v0.1.x / 0.2:\n- File: `jaxmarl/environments/mpe/common.py` — contains `class MPEEnv` base? Actually let me recall. The JaxMARL repo structure:\n  - `jaxmarl/environments/__init__.py` registers `SimpleSpread` from `jaxmarl.environments.mpe.simple_spread`.\n  - `jaxmarl/environments/mpe/__init__.py` exports `SimpleSpread`.\n  - `jaxmarl/environments/mpe/simple_spread.py` — `class SimpleSpread(MPEEnv)` with `__init__`, `reset(key)`, `step_env(key, state, actions)` returning `(obs, state, reward, done, info)`.\n  - `jaxmarl/environments/mpe/common.py` — `class MPEEnv` with `step`, `get_obs`, `get_reward`, `is_terminal`, helper `move`, `post_step`, `pre_step` etc. and `class State` (namedtuple with pos, vel, force, acc, ... or a PyTree dataclass).\n  - `jaxmarl/environments/mpe/rendering.py`.\n\nActually I recall the MPE in JaxMARL was contributed under `jaxmarl/environments/mpe/` with files: `__init__.py`, `common.py`, `simple_spread.py`, `simple_world_comm.py`, `simple_tag.py`, `simple_adversary.py`, `rendering.py`. The `MPEEnv` base is in `common.py`. `Step` returns a `TimeStep` from `jaxmarl/environments/general/import_jax`. JaxMARL uses `dm_env` style `TimeStep`. The `step` function is `@partial(jit, static_argnums=(0,))` in JaxMARL environments? Let me recall: In JaxMARL, `def step(self, key, state, actions, ...)` — I believe JaxMARL env steps are NOT internally jitted; users jit their training loops. However in `jaxmarl/environments/ma_env.py` base `class MultiAgentEnv`, the `step` wraps `_step`? Not sure. I'll flag as uncertainty to verify at implement time.\n\nActually I do recall JaxMARL MPE `simple_spread.py` having something like:\n```python\n@partial(jit, static_argnums=(0,))\ndef step(self, key, state, actions):\n    ...\n    obs, state, rewards, dones, infos = self.step_env(key, state, actions)\n    return obs, state, rewards, dones, infos\n```\nand `reset` similarly. I'm not 100% sure the `@jit` decorator is inside the env. Either way, the design instructs wrapping the benchmark loop's `env.step` call in `jax.jit` explicitly (compile once) to ensure steady-state measurement and to replicate typical usage.\n\nAction space in JaxMARL MPE: I believe it's `spaces.Box(low=-2, high=2, shape=(num_agents, 5), dtype=jnp.float32)` — actually the action space for simple_spread in JaxMARL... Hmm. Let me recall the JaxMARL readme for MPE: \"MPE environments ... discrete action space of size 5, continuous actions also supported.\" I recall `simple_spread.py` has:\n```python\nif self.continuous:\n    self.action_space = spaces.Box(-2, 2, (self.num_agents, 5), jnp.float32)\nelse:\n    self.action_space = spaces.Discrete(5)\n```\nHmm not sure. In PettingZoo MPE there's a `continuous_actions=False` param and `Discrete(5)` action space mapping to the one-hot 5-vector.\n\nI need to be careful not to over-assert. In the plan I'll note: \"confirm the exact action-space representation (Discrete(5) vs continuous 5-vector) of each stack at implementation time and map the shared seeded action stream to each env's native format.\"\n\nPettingZoo MPE (github.com/Farama-Foundation/PettingZoo), versions ~1.24 / 1.25 / 1.26:\n- `pettingzoo/mpe/simple_spread/simple_spread.py`:\n  - `class raw_env(multi_agent_env.MultiAgentEnv, EzPickle)`\n  - `class parallel_env(parallel_env.ParallelEnv, EzPickle)` — the parallel wrapper.\n  - `class env(aec_env.AECEnv, EzPickle)` — AEC wrapper (classic).\n  - `__init__(self, n=3, local_ratio=0.5, max_cycles=25, continuous_actions=False, render_mode=None)`.\n  - `reset()`, `step(action)`, `observe(agent)`, etc.\n  - The underlying mechanics: `self.env` is a `MPEEnv` (from `pettingzoo/mpe/_mpe_env.py`), and `self._seed()` uses `self.env.seed(seed)` → `np.random.seed(seed)`.\n- `pettingzoo/mpe/_mpe_env.py`:\n  - `class MPEEnv` (extends `MP`), `class MP` contains `Agent`, `Landmark`, `Entity`, `World`, `Action`; the physics `World.step(dt=0.1)` implements damping + force → acceleration → velocity → position; `scenario.observation(agent, world)` builds the numpy observation; `scenario.reward(agent, world)` computes reward; `MP.get_actions(observations, action_side_effects)` etc.\n- `pettingzoo/utils/conversions.py` — `parallel_to_aec` wrappers.\n\nKey per-step overhead in PettingZoo parallel_env.step for simple_spread:\n1. `parallel_env.step(actions_dict)` → checks `self.env._check_action(...)`? Actually the parallel env has `action_spaces` and in step, for each agent, `action = self.env.action_space(agent, actions[agent])` — the `MPEEnv.action_space(agent, action)` converts a `Discrete` index into a one-hot 5-vector (\"action translation\"). This conversion loops over agents.\n2. `self.env.step(actions)` → core: computes world step via numpy, then `scenario.observation` per agent, `scenario.reward` per agent, building dicts.\n3. Wrapping into `obs, rewards, dones, infos` dicts, and maintaining `self.agents`, `self.possible_agents`, cumulative rewards.\n\nSo the PettingZoo per-step cost = Python dict bookkeeping + numpy conversions + per-agent Python loops + physics. JaxMARL per-step (jitted) = single traced computation over all agents.\n\nNow the benchmark harness design:\n\nPrimary metric (common across both):\n- \"single-env sequential steps/sec\": time a loop of N steps on a single env instance: `t0=perf_counter(); for t in range(N): s=env.step(...); t1=perf_counter(); sps = N/(t1-t0)`. This includes framework overhead and is the fairest apples-to-apples \"steps per second\" as users experience it.\n\nSecondary metric (JaxMARL only / headroom):\n- \"batched vectorized steps/sec\": vmap step over batch_size (e.g., 32/64/128) envs; sps = batch_size*N/t. Report to show scaling; clearly not comparable to single-env PettingZoo (PettingZoo has no vectorized path), so report separately and don't mix into the headline comparison.\n\nAlternatively, to reduce the JAX small-batch penalty being the *only* story, also include a \"manual batching\" for PettingZoo? You can't vectorize PettingZoo's numpy env easily because the world is a Python object. Could run B parallel PettingZoo envs in separate Python threads/processes and aggregate — that changes the metric. Better to keep: (1) single-env sequential SPS as the headline common metric, (2) optional JaxMARL vmapped SPS, (3) optional PettingZoo \"B parallel single-env processes aggregated SPS\" as a best-case for the CPU stack. But this complicates. Keep it simple: two primary measurements + note.\n\nEpisode length: Simple Spread in both is typically 25 steps/episode (PettingZoo default max_cycles=25; JaxMARL SimpleSpread default `max_steps`?). JaxMARL SimpleSpread `__init__(..., max_steps=25?)` — hmm, actually JaxMARL MPE envs track episode length via a `step_count` in state and `is_terminal` when step_count >= max_steps. I recall JaxMARL SimpleSpread default max_steps=200? Not sure. The design: set both explicitly to the same episode length = 25 steps (PettingZoo default), run for a fixed total of T=5000 steps composed of many episodes, resetting when done. Actually for a pure throughput benchmark we might run episodes contiguously (reset immediately on done) to avoid idle time; but include the resets in the timed region? Resets are part of \"full episode of steps\" if we're honest. Decide: timed region includes resets (since a training run must reset). But for measuring steady-state step throughput, it's cleaner to only time steps. The researcher wants \"steps-per-second under random actions\" — I'll define: SPS = total env steps (including across episode boundaries) / elapsed wall time for the run loop (including resets, excluding warmup and compilation). Both stacks do the same.\n\nWarmup:\n- JaxMARL: JIT compilation of reset+step on first call. Warm up with a separate call before timing: run ~20 steps (and one reset) untimed, so AOT compile completes. Also `jax.jit` explicit; can `lower().compile()` upfront.\n- PettingZoo: no compilation, but give it a few \"warmup\" episodes for cache/CPU frequency ramp, and pin CPU governor? Can't control governor usually. Warmup 2-3 runs then measure.\n- Also do a small number of trial repetition (e.g., 10 trials).\n\nTrials: \n- R = 10 trials (each trial = reset new seed + run N=2000 steps), interleave order (alternate frameworks / block randomization) to control for machine drift (thermal, frequency). Actually to control drift, alternate: for each trial i: measure PG, measure Jax, then next trial. Report per-trial paired differences.\n\nTiming methodology:\n- Use `time.perf_counter()` or `timeit` at the loop boundary (outside the per-step call) — \"loop-level\" timing. For PettingZoo use the parallel_env; for JaxMARL, keep Python loop calling `env.step`. Don't time individual calls (that underestimates loop/interpret overhead). The whole loop is in the timed region except warmup/compile.\n- Single-thread affinity: set `taskset -c 0-3` (or OMP_NUM_THREADS=1) to reduce scheduler noise? But JAX on CPU benefits from threads; for a fair comparison, use same thread config for both: pin to a fixed set of cores (e.g., 4 cores), set `OMP_NUM_THREADS=4`, `XLA_FLAGS=--xla_cpu_multi_thread_eigen`. Better: fix the same environment for both stacks (identical affinity, same thread count) — that's a control, not a way to make them equal; just identical conditions.\n\nRandom actions:\n- Pre-generate the action stream once: seeded `numpy.random.default_rng(2024)` array `A` of shape `(N, num_agents)` with integers in {0..4} (Discrete(5)). Feed:\n  - PettingZoo parallel: `{agent: int(A[t,i]) for i,agent in enumerate(agents)}`.\n  - JaxMARL: convert each integer to the env's action format: if Discrete — `A[t]` as `int32` array of shape (num_agents,); if continuous 5-vector — one-hot encoding of A[t]. Both stacks then process the identical logical action sequence. This removes RNG-handling as a confound (no per-step JAX/NumPy RNG). Alternative \"on-the-fly\" approach adds RNG cost asymmetrically, so pre-generation is cleaner. State this choice.\n\nConfig equivalence:\n- n_agents = 3, n_landmarks = 3.\n- World params: PettingZoo simple_spread defaults: world_dt=0.1, damping=0.25? contact_margin, collision_force... The scenario params (world size 1, default values) should be matched where visible; note we keep each env's own default world constants (they're the same scenario in both, both descended from OpenAI MPE, so defaults should already match) — verify max_speed, dt, damping, reward weights (reward is distance-based + local_ratio=0.5? PettingZoo simple_spread reward uses `scenario.reward` with local_ratio; JaxMARL simple_spread matches). Verification step: after setup, run both envs on same seeded stream and confirm observation shape and per-step reward vectors match (sanity check) — but note if small mismatches exist (e.g., 25 vs some max_steps, reward scale), the benchmark only compares throughput, not rewards, so we mainly need action/obs shapes and episode length to match.\n\nMetric definition:\n- steps_per_second = N_steps / T_elapsed.\n- Report mean ± std across trials, median, 95% CI (bootstrapped), min/max.\n- \"pure per-step (excluding loop)\": also time one unjitted Python call overhead measurement? The researcher explicitly wants \"with and without wall-clock per-step overhead\". So define:\n  - `SPS_end_to_end`: whole-loop wall clock (includes framework step+observe+loop overhead).\n  - `SPS_step_only`: measure only inside `env.step(...)` time (sum of wall time spent inside the step call, obtained by timing each step call and summing) → gives \"pure step-function throughput\". For JaxMARL jitted step on CPU this mostly ~= end-to-end (loop overhead tiny if we keep Python loop thin, but JAX dispatch overhead counted). Actually if we time inside each step call with perf_counter we add overhead to both equally. Cleaner: two separate timed runs per stack, one timing the loop, one timing the sum of per-call durations. State both.\n\nAnalysis plan:\n- Descriptive: mean, median, std, CV, min, max, 95% CI.\n- Comparative: since trials are paired by construction (same machine, interleaved), use paired test: non-parametric Wilcoxon signed-rank (robust) plus paired t-test on log SPS (ratio). Report ratio of medians (speedup) with bootstrap CI of the ratio.\n- Check for drift: order trials interleaved; also do a \"noise floor\" check: measure a trivial empty loop SPS upper bound to contextualize overhead.\n- Feasibility check: \n  - This environment CANNOT execute: Python 3.14.4, no numpy/jax/pettingzoo/gymnasium, no network (cannot pip install), GPU off. So the design targets a prepared host with Python 3.11/3.12, same CPU (224-core x86_64), and pinned wheels.\n  - Pin versions: jaxmarl==0.1.0 (or git @ commit), jax==0.4.x + jaxlib==0.4.x (CPU wheel), pettingzoo==1.24.x, gymnasium==0.29/1.0, numpy==1.26 (for py3.11), and note jaxmarl requires `gymnasium` and `chex`, `jaxmarl` envs need `dm_env`? JaxMARL deps: jax, chex, gymnax? JaxMARL requires `gymnasium`, `chex`, `jax`, `jaxlib`, `flash-attn`(optional), `brax`(optional), `tqdm`. We only need MPE path: jax, jaxlib, numpy, chex (TimeStep?), jaxmarl. PettingZoo MPE needs gymnasium+pettingzoo (v1.x MPE uses gymnasium spaces).\n  - GPU unavailable → JAX on CPU with default xla flags; document that results are CPU-only and GPU would change the JaxMARL story.\n\nUncertainties/assumptions to state:\n- Comparison scope: primary = single-env sequential steps/sec end-to-end (full loop: reset, step, observe within step). Secondary = pure step-call-only throughput for both. Tertiary (contextual, not for head-to-head claim): JaxMARL vmapped batch.\n- Random actions: pre-generated seeded discrete stream consumed identically, expressed in each env's native action format (needs confirming whether JaxMARL SimpleSpread expects Discrete(5) indices or continuous 5-vectors; both stacks share the same OpenAI-MPE-derived action semantics: index selects noop/left/right/up/down).\n- jax.jit: explicitly jit env.step (outside env) for steady state; exclude first-call compile (warmup). This assumes no internal jit conflicts", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport time, os\n# trivial probe: measure a no-op loop to establish the timing tooling works on this host\nimport timeit\nr = timeit.repeat(\"pass\", number=100000, repeat=3)\nprint(\"min no-op loop s/100k:\", min(r))\nprint(\"perf_counter resolution check:\", time.get_clock_info('perf_counter').resolution)\nEOF", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-02e3f09325534eb0bb2e7d2d9835c03a", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-02e3f09325534eb0bb2e7d2d9835c03a", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"python3 - <<'EOF'\\nimport time, os\\n# trivial probe: measure a no-op loop to establish the timing tooling works on this host\\nimport timeit\\nr = timeit.repeat(\\\"pass\\\", number=100000, repeat=3)\\nprint(\\\"min no-op loop s/100k:\\\", min(r))\\nprint(\\\"perf_counter resolution check:\\\", time.get_clock_info('perf_counter').resolution)\\nEOF\", \"summary\": \"Probe timing tooling works on this host\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-886bf9fb55b742f49bce04deaa5ee815", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Probe timing tooling works on this host", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
