{"id": "23bef0d0-41bf-4051-a614-10cd8858e645", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T13:58:26.725160", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "08cfd652-d7a5-4765-859f-a9de29f0f0af", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "I now have everything I need to propose a plan. Here is what I found and my proposed approach.\n\n## Findings from the environment (evidence)\n\n- `/workspace` contains **only `task.json`** (\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"). A full filesystem search (`find / -iname '*jaxmarl*' -o -iname '*pettingzoo*'` and deeper searches) returned **no JaxMARL, PettingZoo, JAX, or MPE source**.\n- **No relevant packages installed**: `numpy`, `scipy`, `jax`, `jaxlib`, `gymnasium`, `pettingzoo`, `torch` are all MISSING (bare system Python 3.14.4).\n- **Network is disabled** (`Temporary failure in name resolution`), so `pip install` cannot fetch anything in this sandbox.\n- Host: x86_64, 224 CPUs, ~2 TB RAM, GPU disabled. Timing tooling works (`time.perf_counter` resolution 1e-9 s; no-op loop probe passes).\n\n**Implication:** the two repos cannot be inspected or executed in this sandbox. The design below is therefore grounded in the canonical upstream layout of both projects (FLAIROx/JaxMARL and Farama-Foundation/PettingZoo, both derived from OpenAI's `multiagent-particle-envs`), with explicit version pins and a verification step at implementation time. I flag below where I am reasoning from knowledge rather than from repo files present here.\n\n## Grounding in the actual repos (from knowledge, to be verified at implementation time)\n\n**JaxMARL MPE Simple Spread** — `jaxmarl/environments/mpe/simple_spread.py` → `class SimpleSpread(MPEEnv)`; base logic in `jaxmarl/environments/mpe/common.py` (`MPEEnv`, `State` pytree, `get_obs`, `get_reward`, `is_terminal`, physics helpers). API: `obs, state = env.reset(key)`; `obs, state, rews, dones, infos = env.step(key, state, actions)`; returns `TimeStep`-style namedtuple in the standard JaxMARL API. All computation is on `jnp` arrays; step is JIT-able and internally vectorized across agents (the whole agent batch is traced as one computation). This is **single-step** (one env instance, one step call per env) — there is no built-in multi-env vmap in the env itself; batching is done by the caller via `jax.vmap`.\n\n**PettingZoo MPE Simple Spread** — `pettingzoo/mpe/simple_spread/simple_spread.py` → `class raw_env(MultiAgentEnv, EzPickle)` with `parallel_env`/`env` (AEC) wrappers; mechanics vendored from OpenAI MPE in `pettingzoo/mpe/_mpe_env.py` (world/agent/landmark physics, `MPEEnv.action_space(agent, action)` action translation, per-agent `scenario.observation`/`scenario.reward`). Per-step overhead comes from: Python dict bookkeeping per agent, Discrete→one-hot action translation (`_mpe_env.action_space`), per-agent numpy observation/reward construction, and small `numpy` world-physics `dt=0.1` updates — all in a Python loop.\n\n## Proposed approach (for your approval)\n\n1. **Primary common metric — single-env sequential steps/sec, end-to-end loop:** time the whole loop `for t in range(N): obs, … = env.step(action_stream[t])` (including resets across episode boundaries) with `time.perf_counter()` at the loop boundary only. This is the apples-to-apples \"how I would actually train\" measure for both stacks.\n2. **Secondary metrics:** (a) *pure step-call-only* SPS for both (sum of per-call wall times, in a separate timed run) — separates framework step function cost from loop/observe overhead; (b) *JaxMARL-only vmapped batch* SPS (batch 32/64/128) reported separately as headroom, *not* mixed into the head-to-head claim (PettingZoo has no vectorized path).\n3. **Random actions:** pre-generate ONE seeded action stream `A` (np rng seed fixed, shape `(N, n_agents)`, integers 0–4), feed the *same logical stream* to both stacks in each env's native format (PettingZoo: `{agent: int(A[t,i])}`; JaxMARL: env-native representation of the same index, Discrete or one-hot 5-vector per the env's `action_space`). This removes RNG handling (JAX vs NumPy streams) as a confound.\n4. **Harness:** explicit `jax.jit(env.step)` for JaxMARL; JIT warmup (compile + ~20 steps) excluded from timing; PettingZoo warmup episodes for frequency/cache ramp; `R=10` interleaved trials (alternate stack order per trial to cancel machine drift), `N=2000` steps/trial, `n_agents=3`, `n_landmarks=3`, episode length matched (PettingZoo default `max_cycles=25`; set JaxMARL's equivalent episode/max_steps to the same value).\n5. **Controls:** identical env config, identical core affinity/thread env (`OMP_NUM_THREADS` and `taskset` pinned identically), identical measurement methodology, common SPS formula `N / (t_end - t_start)`, no GPU.\n6. **Analysis:** mean/median SPS, std, CV, min/max, bootstrap 95% CI per stack; paired analysis (trials are paired by construction) with Wilcoxon signed-rank + paired t-test on log SPS; report median speedup ratio with bootstrap CI; noise-floor check via a trivial empty-loop SPS cap.\n7. **Feasibility:** this sandbox cannot run either stack (Python 3.14.4 with no jaxlib wheels, no deps, no network). Design targets a prepared host with Python 3.11/3.12, the same CPU, and pinned wheels (see below); JAX runs on CPU only (GPU off).\n\nNow let me formally hand off the plan with the consequential choices and open questions.\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "Timing tooling works (perf_counter nanosecond resolution). Now I have all the facts I need to write the plan. Let me summarize findings and submit the plan.\n\nKey findings to report:\n1. /workspace contains only task.json. No jaxmarl/pettingzoo/jax source anywhere on filesystem; no network; no numpy/jax/gymnasium/pettingzoo installed. So I cannot literally inspect the repos — I ground the design in the canonical upstream layout of JaxMARL (FLAIROx/JaxMARL `jaxmarl/environments/mpe/`) and PettingZoo (`pettingzoo/mpe/simple_spread/`) from knowledge, with version pins to fetch at implementation time.\n2. Environment: Python 3.14.4, x86_64, 224 CPUs, ~2TB RAM, GPU off, network off → this sandbox cannot execute either stack (Python 3.14 lacks jaxlib wheels; no deps; no pip). Design targets a prepared host.\n3. Timing tooling present (perf_counter ns resolution) — relevant if a lightweight feasibility probe were desired.\n\nNow compose the plan with the details the researcher asked for. Keep it well-organized and concrete. This is the \"plan\" stage — not the final deliverable. I'll propose the approach + consequential choices, and flag ambiguities to be confirmed by the researcher before the full plan.\n\nLet me draft the answer.\n\nStructure:\n- What I found in the environment (evidence).\n- Grounding: canonical repo layout & function names (with a confidence note).\n- Proposed design:\n  1. Metric definitions (three tiers).\n  2. Harness (warmup, trials, steps, timing).\n  3. Random action policy.\n  4. Controls.\n  5. Analysis plan.\n  6. Feasibility.\n- Consequential assumptions & uncertainties to confirm.\n\nI'll use end_researcher_turn with stage=plan.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## Experiment design plan (for approval — full final plan follows after your review)\n\n### 0. Environment reality check (what I found)\n- `/workspace` contains only `task.json`; no JaxMARL/PettingZoo/JAX source anywhere on the filesystem; network disabled; `numpy/jax/jaxlib/gymnasium/pettingzoo` all missing; system Python 3.14.4 (no jaxlib wheels exist for 3.14), x86_64, 224 cores, ~2 TB RAM, GPU off.\n- ⇒ This sandbox CANNOT execute either stack. The design is grounded in the canonical upstream repo layout (FLAIROx/JaxMARL `jaxmarl/environments/mpe/`; Farama PettingZoo `pettingzoo/mpe/simple_spread/`, vendored OpenAI MPE in `pettingzoo/mpe/_mpe_env.py`) and targets a prepared host with Python 3.11/3.12 + pinned wheels + same CPU. An implementation-time \"verify env layout/action spaces\" step is mandatory.\n\n### 1. Comparison scope (consequential choice)\n- **Headline metric A (common, primary):** single-environment **sequential steps/sec, end-to-end run-loop** — time the entire loop `for t in range(N): obs, rew, done, info = env.step(action_stream[t])` (including reset at episode boundaries) at the loop boundary with `time.perf_counter()`. SPS = N / (t_end − t_start). Captures each framework's real per-step overhead (PettingZoo dict/action-translation/observe overhead; JAX dispatch) as an end user experiences it.\n- **Metric B (per-step-only):** same harness but sum of wall time spent inside the `env.step()` call only (separate timed run) ⇒ \"pure step-function throughput\" for both stacks.\n- **Metric C (JaxMARL-only context):** `jax.vmap(env.step)` over a batch (32/64/128 envs), SPS = batch_size·N / t. Reported separately as scaling headroom; explicitly NOT pooled into the head-to-head claim (PettingZoo has no vectorized path).\n\n### 2. Benchmark harness (identical methodology both stacks)\n- **Config:** n_agents=3, n_landmarks=3; episode length matched explicitly (PettingZoo default max_cycles=25; set JaxMARL episode/max_steps to the same value); all other scenario defaults left at each env's own defaults (both derive from OpenAI MPE so world dt=0.1, damping, max_speed, observation/reward shapes should already match — verify at setup).\n- **Warmup (excluded from timing):** JaxMARL — explicitly `jax.jit(env.step)` (+ `reset`), force AOT compilation (`step.lower().compile()`), then ~20 untimed warmup steps; PettingZoo — 2 untimed warmup episodes for cache/frequency ramp.\n- **Trials:** R=10 trials per metric; each trial = N=2000 contiguous steps across episodes (reset-on-done, resets inside the timed loop for metric A). Interleave stack order per trial (block randomization) to cancel thermal/frequency drift.\n- **Timing:** `time.perf_counter()` at loop boundary; for metric B, accumulate per-call durations; 3 reps of each trial, take the median rep per trial.\n- **Pin resources identically:** same `taskset -c 0..k`, same `OMP_NUM_THREADS`, same `XLA_FLAGS` for both stacks (identity of conditions is the control, not a way to equalize).\n\n### 3. Random action policy\n- Pre-generate ONE seeded stream `A ← numpy.random.default_rng(FIXED_SEED).integers(0,5,size=(N, n_agents))` once; feed the **same logical action indices** to both stacks in each env's native representation (PettingZoo: `{agent: int(A[t,i])}` for Discrete(5); JaxMARL: env-native encoding — Discrete indices or one-hot 5-vector per its `action_space`). This removes JAX-vs-NumPy RNG cost/stream as a confound. (Alternative on-the-fly seeded sampling would add asymmetric RNG overhead — rejected.)\n\n### 4. Controls\n1. Identical env config (agents, landmarks, episode length, obs/action dims — all asserted via runtime shape checks). 2. Identical hardware + identical CPU affinity/thread env. 3. Identical measurement methodology (same timer, same loop harness, same warmup concept). 4. Common metric definition SPS=N/t for A and B; C documented separately. 5. No GPU for either stack. 6. Same action stream. 7. Compilation/warmup excluded. 8. Same Python minor version on the execution host.\n\n### 5. Analysis plan\n- Descriptive: mean, median, std, CV, min/max, bootstrap 95% CI of SPS per stack per metric.\n- Comparative (paired by construction): Wilcoxon signed-rank + paired t-test on log(SPS); report median speedup ratio (Jax/PZ) with bootstrap CI; effect size (Cliff's delta or paired Cohen's d).\n- Robustness: interleaved order to detect drift; report %CV; noise-floor cap via trivial empty-loop SPS (upper bound on achievable loop speed on this host); a sanity check that the two envs produce matching observation shapes and near-matching reward traces on the same action stream (throughput is the headline, but shape/reward parity validates the comparison is about the same environment).\n\n### 6. Feasibility\n- **Blocker (this sandbox):** Python 3.14.4 + no deps + no network ⇒ neither stack runs here; JAX CPU-only since GPU off. Design therefore specifies a target host: Python 3.11.9 venv (jaxlib wheels available), same 224-core x86_64 CPU, internet to `pip install`.\n- **Pinned deps (target host):** `jaxmarl==0.1.0` (or git@commit of FLAIROx/JaxMARL master), `jax==0.4.38` + `jaxlib==0.4.38` (CPU wheel), `pettingzoo==1.24.3`, `gymnasium==0.29.1`, `numpy==1.26.4`, `chex==0.1.86` (jaxmarl dep). Python 3.11.9.\n- **Runtime budget check:** JaxMARL CPU jitted single-env step ≈ tens of µs; PettingZoo step ≈ ~100 µs−1 ms (dict + numpy). N=2000 steps × R=10 trials ≈ seconds per metric — easily fits 60-step budget. C metric optional and cheap.\n\n### Open questions for you to confirm (uncertainties)\n1. **Scope:** agree that headline = single-env sequential end-to-end SPS (A), with B (per-step-only) as the \"without wall-clock overhead\" number, and C (vmapped batch) as JaxMARL-only headroom — i.e., we do NOT try to make PettingZoo \"batched\"?\n2. **JaxMARL steps/sec on CPU:** rely on explicit `jax.jit(env.step)` + AOT warmup (I believe JaxMARL env classes do not jit internally; the user jits their loop). Confirm this is acceptable as the canonical usage.\n3. **Episode/random-action format:** pre-generated Discrete(5) index stream shared by both. This requires JaxMARL SimpleSpread to accept Discrete indices (or we one-hot into its 5-vector) — I'll verify its `action_space` at implementation time; is discrete-index parity acceptable to you?\n4. **Version pins** above acceptable? (Python 3.11.9 target venv, network required at implementation, not in this sandbox.)", "evidence": "Inspected /workspace: contains only task.json (\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"). Filesystem-wide searches for jaxmarl/pettingzoo/jax/mpe returned no source. Network is disabled (DNS resolution failure on pypi.org). None of numpy/scipy/jax/jaxlib/gymnasium/pettingzoo/torch are installed under system Python 3.14.4 (x86_64, 224 cores, ~2TB RAM, GPU disabled). Timing tooling verified working (perf_counter 1e-9s resolution; no-op loop probe passes).", "assumptions": ["The comparison scope is single-environment sequential end-to-end steps/sec as the headline common metric (PettingZoo and JaxMARL as users actually step a single env); vmapped batching is a JaxMARL-only headroom metric, not mixed into the head-to-head claim.", "JaxMARL MPE SimpleSpread is run with an explicit jax.jit(env.step) on CPU, with compilation/warmup excluded from timing; JAX runs purely on CPU in this environment (GPU off).", "'Random actions' means one pre-generated, seeded Discrete(5) index stream consumed identically by both stacks (encoded into each env's native action format), eliminating JAX-vs-NumPy RNG as a confound.", "Both environments are the same OpenAI-MPE-derived Simple Spread scenario, so world constants and observation/reward shapes already match; parity is verified at setup.", "Neither stack can run in this sandbox (Python 3.14.4, no deps, no network); the design targets a prepared Python 3.11 host with pinned wheels.", "Version pins for the execution host: jaxmarl==0.1.0, jax/jaxlib==0.4.38 CPU, pettingzoo==1.24.3, gymnasium==0.29.1, numpy==1.26.4, chex==0.1.86, Python 3.11.9."], "uncertainties": ["Exact JaxMARL MPE action representation (Discrete(5) indices vs continuous 5-vector) and whether jaxmarl env classes jit internally — to be confirmed by reading jaxmarl/environments/mpe/simple_spread.py and common.py at implementation time.", "Exact default max_steps/episode length of JaxMARL SimpleSpread vs PettingZoo max_cycles=25 — will be pinned to the same value explicitly.", "CPU-only JIT dispatch/threading overhead on the 224-core host is unknown a priori; interleaving and the empty-loop noise floor will bound its impact.", "Wheel availability for jaxlib on the target Python version at implementation time (pin adjusts within 0.4.x if needed)."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_t