{"id": "a3d3581d-9ddc-4dba-bca4-61d2ad97d14e", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T13:58:54.407363", "source": "user", "parent_id": "d5d2e639-ea7d-4498-81aa-5a63747040fa", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approved — proceed to draft the final deliverable. Your sandbox reality check (only task.json present, no repo source, no deps, no network, Python 3.14, GPU off) and your decision to ground the design in the canonical upstream layouts (FLAIROx/JaxMARL jaxmarl/environments/mpe/; Farama pettingzoo/mpe/simple_spread/) are accepted, with an in-plan verification step. Answers to your four open questions:\\n\\n1. Scope — Yes: headline = single-environment sequential end-to-end loop SPS (metric A); metric B (per-step-only) reported as context; metric C (JaxMARL-only vmapped batch) reported as headroom and explicitly NOT pooled into the head-to-head claim.\\n2. JIT — Yes: rely on explicit jax.jit(env.step) + AOT warmup (compilation excluded from timing) as canonical usage; at implementation check whether jaxmarl jits internally and document the wrapping accordingly (redundant double-jit is harmless but should be stated).\\n3. Action format — Yes: pre-generated seeded Discrete(5) index stream shared by both stacks is acceptable; specify in the plan exactly how indexes encode into JaxMARL's native action tensor (one-hot 5-vector if that is its space) and assert equal logical content on both stacks.\\n4. Versions — Yes: pins acceptable; keep numpy at a jaxlib-compatible version and allow jax/jaxlib patch-level adjustment within 0.4.x if the target-Python wheel set requires it.\\n\\nTwo refinements to fold into the final plan before submission:\\n- Add a \\\"Known limitations\\\" section: JAX runs CPU-only here (GPU off); PettingZoo has no vectorized path so C is context only; N=2000/R=10 is indicative, not a high-power benchmark; and per-step timer overhead can inflate the measured step-only cost of the slower loop — so keep A as the headline and treat B/C as qualitative context.\\n- Make the plan self-contained for execution elsewhere: exact environment setup commands and env-var sets, runner-script layout, the runtime observation-shape/reward-trace parity assertions, and an explicit statement that this is a design document and no experiment was executed in this sandbox.\\n\\nApproved facts (setup_4 JaxMARL JAX/GPU-accelerated; setup_5 PettingZoo MPE CPU-based; setup_9 MPE Simple Spread the fixed environment) are incorporated into the design's framing and controls.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "92923f85-4e3e-442d-a63a-841922c1b6ed", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T13:59:41.532647", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "a3d3581d-9ddc-4dba-bca4-61d2ad97d14e", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "The plan is approved and all four open questions are resolved. I'll now assemble the complete, self-contained final design document, folding in the requested refinements (known limitations, execution-ready setup, parity assertions, and the design-only/no-execution disclaimer).\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher has approved the plan and given me refinements to fold into the final deliverable:\n1. Known limitations section (CPU-only JAX, C is context only, indicative power, per-step timer overhead caveat, A is headline).\n2. Self-contained for execution elsewhere: environment setup commands + env-var sets, runner-script layout, runtime observation-shape/reward-trace parity assertions, explicit statement that this is a design document and no experiment was executed.\n\nAlso incorporate approved facts:\n- setup_4: JaxMARL JAX/GPU-accelerated\n- setup_5: PettingZoo MPE CPU-based\n- setup_9: MPE Simple Spread the fixed environment\n\nLet me write the final comprehensive design document as the result handoff. I should be thorough but organized. Let me draft it.\n\nStructure:\n1. Title & task statement\n2. Scope & metric definitions (A, B, C)\n3. Grounding in repos (file paths/function names) + verification step\n4. Environment setup (target host) — commands, env vars\n5. Benchmark harness — runner script layout, warmup, trials, timing\n6. Random action policy\n7. Controls\n8. Analysis plan\n9. Feasibility\n10. Known limitations\n11. Assumptions vs fixed requirements\n12. Statement: design only, no experiment executed\n\nI should include concrete runner-script layout (pseudocode/Python skeleton), parity assertions code, setup commands. Let me write this as the `answer` in end_researcher_turn with stage=result, evidence summarizing observations, assumptions, uncertainties.\n\nLet me write detailed content.\n\nEnvironment setup commands (target host, Python 3.11):\n```\npython3.11 -m venv /opt/mpe_bench\nsource /opt/mpe_bench/bin/activate\npip install -U pip\npip install numpy==1.26.4 jax==0.4.38 jaxlib==0.4.38 chex==0.1.86 gymnasium==0.29.1 pettingzoo==1.24.3 jaxmarl==0.1.0\n```\n(allow jax/jaxlib patch within 0.4.x if wheel set requires; numpy pinned to a jaxlib-compatible version — jaxlib 0.4.38 works with numpy 1.26 on py3.11.)\n\nEnv vars for both stacks (identical):\n```\nOMP_NUM_THREADS=4\ntaskset -c 0-3   # same affinity for both stacks\nXLA_FLAGS=\"--xla_cpu_multi_thread_eigen --xla_cpu_fusion=... \"  (keep default; just set thread pool)\n```\nActually for JAX single-env step on CPU, threading may not help tiny graphs; setting OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 and taskset to 4 cores provides identical conditions. Use same env for both.\n\nRunner script layout:\n```\nbench/\n  bench_common.py      # metric A/B timing harness shared skeleton, timing utilities\n  actions.py           # pre-generated stream generator & encoders: pz_actions(), jaxmarl_actions()\n  run_pettingzoo.py    # run_metricA_pz(), run_metricB_pz()\n  run_jaxmarl.py       # run_metricA_jax(), run_metricB_jax(), run_metricC_jax(batch)\n  parity_check.py      # obs-shape & reward-trace parity assertions\n  driver.py            # interleaved trial orchestration, saves results JSON/CSV\n  analyze.py           # stats + bootstrap CI + paired tests + report\n```\n\nTiming code skeleton (metric A):\n```python\nimport time\nt0 = time.perf_counter()\nn_steps = 0\nfor t in range(N):\n    actions = next_actions()\n    obs, rew, done, info = step(actions)   # env-native\n    n_steps += 1\n    if done_all: reset and continue        # reset inside timed region\nt1 = time.perf_counter()\nsps = n_steps / (t1 - t0)\n```\nMetric B: accumulate `dt_step += time.perf_counter() - tstep` around each step call.\n\nParity assertions:\n- obs shape per agent: Simple Spread with 3 agents/3 landmarks → observation dim: each agent sees: relative positions of other agents (2*(n-1)) + relative positions of landmarks (2*n) + own velocity (2) + (landmark states?) — OpenAI simple_spread observation = [vel, pos_rel_others, land_rel, land_vel?]. Actually simple_spread observation: concatenation over: own velocity (2), positions of other agents relative (2*(n-1)), relative positions of landmarks (2*n). That's 2 + 4 + 6 = 12? Let me recall: simple_spread agent obs in OpenAI: \n  ```\n  obs = [self.world.dt? no]\n  if agent.movable: obs.append(agent.state.p_vel)\n  for other in world.agents: if other is agent: continue; obs.append(relative_position(agent, other))\n  for entity in world.landmarks: obs.append(relative_position(agent, entity))\n  ```\n  So dim = 2 + 2*(n-1) + 2*n = 2 + 4 + 6 = 12 for n=3. Yes 12.\n- Action space per agent: Discrete(5) in PettingZoo; JaxMARL: 5-dim vector (or Discrete(5)). Assert per-agent action dim 5 and obs dim 12 in both, for n_agents=3, n_landmarks=3.\n- Reward parity: on same action stream, per-step summed rewards should match to tolerance (both should produce identical distance-based rewards since same scenario). Assert |r_pz - r_jax| < 1e-6 per step (or assert correlation ≥ 0.99 and mean abs diff < small) — allow a small tolerance because JaxMARL may implement tie-breaking or dt slightly differently. This is a sanity check, not a claim.\n\nEpisode length: set both to same L (e.g., 25). PettingZoo parallel_env has `max_cycles=25`. JaxMARL SimpleSpread: set `max_steps`? I recall JaxMARL MPE envs may not implement a hard max_steps internally in the same way — some JaxMARL MPE envs run indefinitely and the training loop caps episodes. To be safe: in the harness, treat episode termination as the env's `done`/terminal signal; for the throughput run we reset on done; for parity run we force both to reset at exactly L=25 steps regardless (to keep timing comparison structural). Actually cleaner: define episode length L=25 in the harness for both (reset every 25 steps for metric A, whether or not env internally signals done). This makes the benchmark's control (equal reset cadence) explicit and avoids reliance on possibly-different internal max_cycles semantics. I'll state that.\n\nNow feasibility of the measurement in budget: The design-only task; but include a \"feasible checks\" section: parity script + small smoke run (N=100, 1 trial) before the full N=2000 × 10.\n\nStatistical tests details:\n- Per stack: mean, median, std, CV, min, max, bootstrap 95% CI (percentile, 10k resamples).\n- Paired: trials interleaved so (sps_pz_i, sps_jax_i) pairs; Wilcoxon signed-rank on absolute differences; paired t-test on log-transformed SPS (ratio interpretation); median speedup = median(sps_jax/sps_pz) with bootstrap CI via paired resampling; effect size: paired Cohen's d (on log scale) and Cliff's delta.\n- Report decision rule: e.g., if 95% CI of ratio excludes 1.0 and p<0.05, declare a significant difference; otherwise report inconclusive (indicative power).\n\nKnown limitations:\n- CPU-only JAX (GPU off in sandbox and target CPU-only) — speedup measured here is CPU-specific; GPU would change JaxMARL numbers.\n- PettingZoo has no vectorized path; metric C is context only.\n- N=2000 × R=10 is indicative, not high-power.\n- Per-step timer overhead inflates measured step-only cost of slower loop — keep A as headline; B qualitative.\n- Python 3.14.4 in sandbox incompatible; design assumes Python 3.11 target host.\n- Both repos not present in sandbox; grounding from canonical upstream; verify at implementation.\n\nAssumptions vs fixed requirements:\nFixed task requirements: compare JaxMARL vs PettingZoo MPE Simple Spread steps/sec under random actions; design-only (no execution); ground in repos (verification step); approved facts: setup_4 (JaxMARL JAX/GPU-accelerated), setup_5 (PettingZoo MPE CPU-based), setup_9 (MPE Simple Spread fixed env).\nAssumptions (my choices, approved): metric A headline, B/C context; explicit jit; pre-generated seeded discrete stream; target-host pins; Python 3.11; equality of scenario constants.\n\nNow compose final result document. I'll write it fully in the answer field, keep evidence and uncertainties.\n\nLet me write the runner pseudocode concretely enough to be \"self-contained for execution elsewhere.\"", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Final Experiment Design\n## \"How does JaxMARL's MPE Simple Spread compare to the original CPU-based PettingZoo MPE Simple Spread in steps-per-second under random actions?\"\n\n**STATUS: design document only. No experiment was executed in this sandbox.** This sandbox contains only `task.json` (design-only per the task), no repo source, no deps, no network, GPU off, system Python 3.14.4. The design is grounded in the canonical upstream layouts of FLAIROx/JaxMARL (`jaxmarl/environments/mpe/`) and Farama PettingZoo (`pettingzoo/mpe/simple_spread/`, both descended from OpenAI's `multiagent-particle-envs`), with an implementation-time verification step. Approved framing facts are baked in: **setup_4** JaxMARL is JAX/GPU-accelerated, **setup_5** PettingZoo MPE is CPU-based, **setup_9** MPE Simple Spread is the fixed comparison environment.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Comparison scope & metric definitions\n\nThree metrics, defined on the same 3-agent/3-landmark Simple Spread environment:\n\n- **Metric A (HEADLINE, common): single-env sequential end-to-end loop SPS.**\n  `SPS_A = N_steps / (t_end − t_start)` where the timed region is the entire run-loop\n  `for t in range(N): obs, rew, done, info = env.step(actions(t))` including resets at episode boundaries, timed once at the loop boundary with `time.perf_counter()`. This is the apples-to-apples \"as actually used in training\" throughput: it captures PettingZoo's per-step dict/action-translation/observe overhead and JAX's CPU dispatch overhead.\n- **Metric B (context): pure step-function SPS.** Same harness, but wall time accumulated only inside each `env.step(...)` call (separate timed run). Separates \"framework step function cost\" from \"loop + observe bookkeeping\".\n- **Metric C (context, JaxMARL-only headroom): vmapped batch SPS.** `jax.vmap(env.step)` over a batch of B envs (B = 32, 64, 128), `SPS_C = B·N / t`. Reported separately; **explicitly not pooled** into the A-metric head-to-head claim because PettingZoo has no vectorized path.\n\n**Headline claim is made on Metric A only; B and C are qualitative context.**\n\n## 2. Grounding in the actual repositories (verify at implementation)\n\n- **JaxMARL Simple Spread**: `jaxmarl/environments/mpe/simple_spread.py` → `class SimpleSpread(MPEEnv)`; base physics/observation/reward helpers in `jaxmarl/environments/mpe/common.py` (`MPEEnv`, `State` pytree, `get_obs`, `get_reward`, `is_terminal`). API: `obs, state = env.reset(key)`; `step(key, state, actions) -> (obs, state, rews, dones, infos)`, `jnp` throughout, single-step (one env instance per call — no built-in vmap), vectorized across agents inside the traced computation. **Verify at implementation**: whether `step`/`reset` carry an internal `@jit`; if so, our explicit `jax.jit` wrap is redundant-but-harmless and this is documented.\n- **PettingZoo Simple Spread**: `pettingzoo/mpe/simple_spread/simple_spread.py` → `class raw_env(MultiAgentEnv, EzPickle)` with `parallel_env` (ParallelEnv) wrapper; OpenAI-MPE mechanics vendored in `pettingzoo/mpe/_mpe_env.py`: `MPEEnv`, `MP`, `World.step(dt=0.1)`, per-agent `scenario.observation`/`scenario.reward`, and `MPEEnv.action_space(agent, action)` which translates a `Discrete(5)` index to a one-hot `numpy` 5-vector. Per-step overhead = Python dict bookkeeping per agent + Discrete→one-hot translation + per-agent numpy obs/reward construction + numpy world physics, all in Python.\n- **Use the `parallel_env` wrapper** (dict-of-agents API) for PettingZoo — it is the closest structural analog to JaxMARL's vectorized-step API and avoids the extra AEC `step(agent)` dispatch loop, which would stack the deck against PettingZoo for reasons unrelated to MPE.\n\n## 3. Host environment setup (self-contained, for execution elsewhere)\n\nThe sandbox cannot run this (Python 3.14.4, no deps, no network). Target a prepared host with **Python 3.11.9** (jaxlib has wheels), same x86_64 CPU, internet available once at install time.\n\n```bash\npython3.11 -m venv /opt/mpe_bench && source /opt/mpe_bench/bin/activate\npip install -U pip\npip install \\\n  numpy==1.26.4 \\\n  jax==0.4.38 jaxlib==0.4.38 \\        # CPU wheel; allow patch-level bump within 0.4.x if the py3.11 wheel set requires it\n  chex==0.1.86 \\\n  gymnasium==0.29.1 \\\n  pettingzoo==1.24.3 \\\n  jaxmarl==0.1.0\n```\n\nIdentical runtime env for BOTH stacks (identity of conditions is the control):\n```bash\nexport OMP_NUM_THREADS=4\ntaskset -c 0-3        # pin the same 4 cores for both stacks\n# JAX: default XLA CPU flags; report XLA_FLAGS used so they are identical in both runs\n```\n\n## 4. Benchmark harness (identical methodology, both stacks)\n\n**Runner script layout** (`bench/`):\n- `actions.py` — seeded action-stream generation + per-stack encoders.\n- `parity_check.py` — runtime shape + reward-trace parity assertions (see §6).\n- `run_pettingzoo.py`, `run_jaxmarl.py` — metric A/B loops; metric C in `run_jaxmarl.py`.\n- `driver.py` — interleaved trial orchestration; writes `results.json` / `results.csv`.\n- `analyze.py` — statistics + CI + paired tests + markdown report.\n\n**Harness parameters:** `N=2000` steps/trial, `R=10` trials, episode length **L=25** fixed by the harness for both stacks (reset every 25 steps regardless of any internal `max_cycles`/`max_steps` difference — an explicit equal-reset-cadence control), 3 timing reps per trial keeping the median. Resets are inside the timed region for Metric A (a training run must reset).\n\n**Warmup (excluded from timing):** JaxMARL — explicit `jax.jit(env.step)` and `jax.jit(env.reset)`, force AOT compile (`step.lower().compile()`), then 20 untimed warmup steps; PettingZoo — 2 untimed warmup episodes (cache/frequency ramp). Verify at implementation that compile happens before `t_start`.\n\n**Metric-A loop skeleton (shared by both stacks):**\n```python\nt0 = time.perf_counter(); n = 0\nfor t in range(N):\n    a = encode(stream[t])                # env-native actions for this stack\n    obs, rew, dones, info = step(a)      # env-native step call\n    n += 1\n    if (t % L) == (L-1):\n        obs = reset()                    # reset every L steps, inside timed region\nt1 = time.perf_counter()\nsps = n / (t1 - t0)\n```\n**Metric B:** same loop but accumulate `dt_step += time.perf_counter() − t_step` around each `step(a)` call; `sps_b = n / dt_step`.\n\n## 5. Random action policy\nOne pre-generated seeded stream: `A ← numpy.random.default_rng(20240418).integers(0, 5, size=(N_total, n_agents))` — Discrete(5) indices ∈ {noop, left, right, up, down} per agent. Both stacks consume the **same logical indices**:\n- PettingZoo: `{agent: int(A[t, i]) for i, agent in enumerate(agents)}` (Discrete(5) action space).\n- JaxMARL: encode the same index into its native action tensor — one-hot 5-vector `jnp.eye(5)[A[t]]` of shape `(n_agents, 5)` (verify JaxMARL SimpleSpread's `action_space`; if it is Discrete(5) use `A[t]` as int32 indices directly). Assert the two encodings carry identical logical actions (one-hot index == Discrete index). Pre-generating offline removes JAX-vs-NumPy RNG cost/stream as a confound vs. on-the-fly seeded sampling (rejected).\n\n## 6. Controls & parity assertions\nControls: (1) identical env config (n_agents=3, n_landmarks=3, L=25, equal obs/action dims asserted); (2) identical hardware + identical core affinity/thread env + identical XLA/system env vars; (3) identical timing methodology (same timer, same loop harness, same warmup concept, resets included identically); (4) common metric formula SPS=N/t; (5) no GPU for either stack (JAX on CPU); (6) same logical action stream; (7) compilation/warmup excluded; (8) same Python minor version.\n\n**Runtime parity checks** in `parity_check.py` (run once before benchmarking; both are sanity checks, throughput is the headline claim):\n- Observation shape per agent = 12 for 3 agents/3 landmarks (own velocity 2 + relative positions of 2 other agents (2·2) + relative positions of 3 landmarks (2·3)); assert `obs.shape == (num_agents, 12)` in both stacks and action dim == 5.\n- Reward trace: on the same 200-step action stream, per-step summed reward must match within tolerance (|r_jax − r_pz| < 1e-4 per step or Pearson r ≥ 0.99), confirming both stacks implement the same Simple Spread objective.\n\n## 7. Analysis plan\n- **Descriptive** (per stack, per metric): mean, median, std, CV, min/max, bootstrap 95% CI (10k percentile resamples).\n- **Comparative (paired by construction — interleaved trials):** Wilcoxon signed-rank on per-trial differences + paired t-test on log-transformed SPS (interpretable as a ratio); median speedup ratio `median(SPS_jax/SPS_pz)` with bootstrap CI (paired resampling); effect sizes: paired Cohen's d (log scale) and Cliff's delta.\n- **Decision rule applied to Metric A only:** if the bootstrap 95% CI of the ratio excludes 1.0 and p < 0.05, report a significant difference; otherwise report \"indicative, inconclusive\" (see limitations).\n- **Robustness:** interleaved order; report %CV; report empty-loop SPS (trivial `pass` loop) as the per-framework-loop overhead floor / measurement ceiling; report B and C as qualitative context with the A/B gap showing per-step overhead share.\n\n## 8. Feasibility\n- **Feasible here?** No — the sandbox is Python 3.14.4 with no numpy/jax/gymnasium/pettingzoo and no network (cannot pip install); GPU off, so JAX would be CPU-only in any case. The design targets the Python 3.11 host above (§3).\n- **Runtime budget:** on CPU, a jitted JaxMARL single-env step is ~tens of µs and a PettingZoo simple_spread step is ~0.1–1 ms; Metric A at N=2000 × R=10 is seconds per stack — trivially within budget. Feasible-check sequence at implementation: (1) install + import both; (2) `parity_check.py` passes; (3) smoke run N=100, 1 trial per stack; (4) full N=2000 × R=10; then analyze.\n- **Dependency risk:** jaxlib wheel availability for the target Python at install time (mitigate: pin within 0.4.x); jaxmarl 0.1.0's dependency surface (chex) — only the MPE path is used.\n\n## 9. Known limitations\n1. **CPU-only JAX:** GPU is unavailable (setup_4 says JaxMARL is *GPU-accelerated*, but this measurement is CPU-only); reported JaxMARL numbers are CPU-specific and GPU would change them.\n2. **Metric C is context only:** PettingZoo has no vectorized path, so C is never pooled into the head-to-head.\n3. **Indicative power:** N=2000 × R=10 is a lightweight benchmark, not a high-power study; the decision rule (§7) states this explicitly.\n4. **Per-step timer overhead:** timing every call in Metric B adds instrumented-loop overhead that can inflate the measured step-only cost of the slower stack — hence A is the headline and B is qualitative.\n5. **Grounding:** repos not present in sandbox; layout/APIs cited from canonical upstream and verified at implementation; small mismatches (e.g., JaxMARL internal jit, action-space encoding) are resolved by the verification step, not assumed away.\n\n## 10. Assumptions (my choices, approved) vs fixed requirements\n**Fixed requirements:** answer the SPS question for MPE Simple Spread under random actions, design-only (no execution claimed), grounded in the repos with a verification step; setup_4/setup_5/setup_9 framing facts.\n**Assumptions:** Metric A is the headline claim; B and C are context; explicit `jax.jit(env.step)` + AOT warmup is canonical usage; action stream is a pre-generated seeded Discrete(5) index stream encoded natively by each stack; episode length pinned to L=25 by the harness; target host Python 3.11.9 with pinned wheels (numpy 1.26.4, jax/jaxlib 0.4.38 CPU, jaxmarl 0.1.0, pettingzoo 1.24.3, gymnasium 0.29.1, chex 0.1.86); identical core affinity/thread env for both stacks; JAX CPU-only.", "evidence": "Sandbox inspection: /workspace contains only task.json (explicitly design-only, \"repositories and compute are not required\"); filesystem-wide searches (find -iname '*jaxmarl*'/*pettingzoo*/*mpe* and deeper) found no relevant source; network disabled (pypi.org DNS resolution failure); none of numpy/scipy/jax/jaxlib/gymnasium/pettingzoo/torch importable under system Python 3.14.4 (x86_64, 224 cores, ~2TB RAM, GPU off); time.perf_counter verified at 1e-9 s resolution with a working no-op probe. Design grounded in canonical upstream layouts: JaxMARL jaxmarl/environments/mpe/simple_spread.py (class SimpleSpread) + common.py (MPEEnv, get_obs/get_reward); PettingZoo pettingzoo/mpe/simple_spread/simple_spread.py (raw_env/parallel_env) with vendored mechanics in pettingzoo/mpe/_mpe_env.py (MPEEnv.action_space Discrete->one-hot translation, World.step dt=0.1, per-agent scenario.observation/reward) — all to be verified at implementation time since repos are not present locally.", "assumptions": ["Metric A (single-env sequential end-to-end loop SPS) is the headline head-to-head claim; Metrics B (per-step-only) and C (JaxMARL vmapped batch) are qualitative context only.", "JaxMARL SimpleSpread is run with explicit jax.jit(env.step) + AOT-compile warmup on CPU (GPU off); if the env jits internally the redundant double-jit is documented as harmless.", "Random actions = one pre-generated seeded Discrete(5) index stream consumed identically by both stacks, encoded into each env's native action tensor (one-hot 5-vector for JaxMARL if that is its space) with equal logical content asserted.", "Episode length pinned to L=25 by the harness for both stacks (equal reset cadence), independent of any internal max_cycles/max_steps semantics.", "Execution targets a Python 3.11.9 host with network at install time and pinned wheels: numpy 1.26.4, jax/jaxlib 0.4.38 CPU (patch-level within 0.4.x allowed), jaxmarl 0.1.0, pettingzoo 1.24.3, gymnasium 0.29.1, chex 0.1.86; identical CPU affinity (taskset -c 0-3) and OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 for both stacks.", "Both stacks implement the same OpenAI-MPE-derived Simple Spread scenario, so obs shape (12 per agent) and reward semantics match; verified at runtime by parity_check.py with tolerance.", "No experiment was executed in this sandbox; the deliverable is a design document."], "uncertainties": ["Exact JaxMARL MPE action representation (Discrete(5) vs continuous 5-vector) and any internal @jit in simple_spread.py/common.py — resolved by the implementation-time verification step, not assumed.", "jaxlib wheel availability for the target Python at install time — mitigated by pinning within jax/jaxlib 0.4.x.", "CPU-only JAX dispatch/threading behavior on the 224-core x86_64 host — managed via fixed affinity, interleaved trials, and the empty-loop noise floor.", "Whether JaxMARL observation/reward traces match PettingZoo exactly vs to tolerance — parity is asserted as a sanity check (pass/fail gate) rather than a hard equivalence requirement for the throughput claim."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-3830a4180f4549e4b75cd7d467883395", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-3830a4180f4549e4b75cd7d467883395", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"stage\": \"result\", \"summary\": \"Deliver complete experiment design comparing JaxMARL vs PettingZoo MPE Simple Spread SPS\", \"answer\": \"# Final Experiment Design\\n## \\\"How does JaxMARL's MPE Simple Spread compare to the original CPU-based PettingZoo MPE Simple Spread in steps-per-second under random actions?\\\"\\n\\n**STATUS: design document only. No experiment was executed in this sandbox.** This sandbox contains only `task.json` (design-only per the task), no repo source, no deps, no network, GPU off, system Python 3.14.4. The design is grounded in the canonical upstream layouts of FLAIROx/JaxMARL (`jaxmarl/environments/mpe/`) and Farama PettingZoo (`pettingzoo/mpe/simple_spread/`, both descended from OpenAI's `multiagent-particle-envs`), with an implementation-time verification step. Approved framing facts are baked in: **setup_4** JaxMARL is JAX/GPU-accelerated, **setup_5** PettingZoo MPE is CPU-based, **setup_9** MPE Simple Spread is the fixed comparison environment.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Comparison scope & metric definitions\\n\\nThree metrics, defined on the same 3-agent/3-landmark Simple Spread environment:\\n\\n- **Metric A (HEADLINE, common): single-env sequential end-to-end loop SPS.**\\n  `SPS_A = N_steps / (t_end \\u2212 t_start)` where the timed region is the entire run-loop\\n  `for t in range(N): obs, rew, done, info = env.step(actions(t))` including resets at episode boundaries, timed once at the loop boundary with `time.perf_counter()`. This is the apples-to-apples \\\"as actually used in training\\\" throughput: it captures PettingZoo's per-step dict/action-translation/observe overhead and JAX's CPU dispatch overhead.\\n- **Metric B (context): pure step-function SPS.** Same harness, but wall time accumulated only inside each `env.step(...)` call (separate timed run). Separates \\\"framework step function cost\\\" from \\\"loop + observe bookkeeping\\\".\\n- **Metric C (context, JaxMARL-only headroom): vmapped batch SPS.** `jax.vmap(env.step)` over a batch of B envs (B = 32, 64, 128), `SPS_C = B\\u00b7N / t`. Reported separately; **explicitly not pooled** into the A-metric head-to-head claim because PettingZoo has no vectorized path.\\n\\n**Headline claim is made on Metric A only; B and C are qualitative context.**\\n\\n## 2. Grounding in the actual repositories (verify at implementation)\\n\\n- **JaxMARL Simple Spread**: `jaxmarl/environments/mpe/simple_spread.py` \\u2192 `class SimpleSpread(MPEEnv)`; base physics/observation/reward helpers in `jaxmarl/environments/mpe/common.py` (`MPEEnv`, `State` pytree, `get_obs`, `get_reward`, `is_terminal`). API: `obs, state = env.reset(key)`; `step(key, state, actions) -> (obs, state, rews, dones, infos)`, `jnp` throughout, single-step (one env instance per call \\u2014 no built-in vmap), vectorized across agents inside the traced computation. **Verify at implementation**: whether `step`/`reset` carry an internal `@jit`; if so, our explicit `jax.jit` wrap is redundant-but-harmless and this is documented.\\n- **PettingZoo Simple Spread**: `pettingzoo/mpe/simple_spread/simple_spread.py` \\u2192 `class raw_env(MultiAgentEnv, EzPickle)` with `parallel_env` (ParallelEnv) wrapper; OpenAI-MPE mechanics vendored in `pettingzoo/mpe/_mpe_env.py`: `MPEEnv`, `MP`, `World.step(dt=0.1)`, per-agent `scenario.observation`/`scenario.reward`, and `MPEEnv.action_space(agent, action)` which translates a `Discrete(5)` index to a one-hot `numpy` 5-vector. Per-step overhead = Python dict bookkeeping per agent + Discrete\\u2192one-hot translation + per-agent numpy obs/reward construction + numpy world physics, all in Python.\\n- **Use the `parallel_env` wrapper** (dict-of-agents API) for PettingZoo \\u2014 it is the closest structural analog to JaxMARL's vectorized-step API and avoids the extra AEC `step(agent)` dispatch loop, which would stack the deck against PettingZoo for reasons unrelated to MPE.\\n\\n## 3. Host environment setup (self-contained, for execution elsewhere)\\n\\nThe sandbox cannot run this (Python 3.14.4, no deps, no network). Target a prepared host with **Python 3.11.9** (jaxlib has wheels), same x86_64 CPU, internet available once at install time.\\n\\n```bash\\npython3.11 -m venv /opt/mpe_bench && source /opt/mpe_bench/bin/activate\\npip install -U pip\\npip install \\\\\\n  numpy==1.26.4 \\\\\\n  jax==0.4.38 jaxlib==0.4.38 \\\\        # CPU wheel; allow patch-level bump within 0.4.x if the py3.11 wheel set requires it\\n  chex==0.1.86 \\\\\\n  gymnasium==0.29.1 \\\\\\n  pettingzoo==1.24.3 \\\\\\n  jaxmarl==0.1.0\\n```\\n\\nIdentical runtime env for BOTH stacks (identity of conditions is the control):\\n```bash\\nexport OMP_NUM_THREADS=4\\ntaskset -c 0-3        # pin the same 4 cores for both stacks\\n# JAX: default XLA CPU flags; report XLA_FLAGS used so they are identical in both runs\\n```\\n\\n## 4. Benchmark harness (identical methodology, both stacks)\\n\\n**Runner script layout** (`bench/`):\\n- `actions.py` \\u2014 seeded action-stream generation + per-stack encoders.\\n- `parity_check.py` \\u2014 runtime shape + reward-trace parity assertions (see \\u00a76).\\n- `run_pettingzoo.py`, `run_jaxmarl.py` \\u2014 metric A/B loops; metric C in `run_jaxmarl.py`.\\n- `driver.py` \\u2014 interleaved trial orchestration; writes `results.json` / `results.csv`.\\n- `analyze.py` \\u2014 statistics + CI + paired tests + markdown report.\\n\\n**Harness parameters:** `N=2000` steps/trial, `R=10` trials, episode length **L=25** fixed by the harness for both stacks (reset every 25 steps regardless of any internal `max_cycles`/`max_steps` difference \\u2014 an explicit equal-reset-cadence control), 3 timing reps per trial keeping the median. Resets are inside the timed region for Metric A (a training run must reset).\\n\\n**Warmup (excluded from timing):** JaxMARL \\u2014 explicit `jax.jit(env.step)` and `jax.jit(env.reset)`, force AOT compile (`step.lower().compile()`), then 20 untimed warmup steps; PettingZoo \\u2014 2 untimed warmup episodes (cache/frequency ramp). Verify at implementation that compile happens before `t_start`.\\n\\n**Metric-A loop skeleton (shared by both stacks):**\\n```python\\nt0 = time.perf_counter(); n = 0\\nfor t in range(N):\\n    a = encode(stream[t])                # env-native actions for this stack\\n    obs, rew, dones, info = step(a)      # env-native step call\\n    n += 1\\n    if (t % L) == (L-1):\\n        obs = reset()                    # reset every L steps, inside timed region\\nt1 = time.perf_counter()\\nsps = n / (t1 - t0)\\n```\\n**Metric B:** same loop but accumulate `dt_step += time.perf_counter() \\u2212 t_step` around each `step(a)` call; `sps_b = n / dt_step`.\\n\\n## 5. Random action policy\\nOne pre-generated seeded stream: `A \\u2190 numpy.random.default_rng(20240418).integers(0, 5, size=(N_total, n_agents))` \\u2014 Discrete(5) indices \\u2208 {noop, left, right, up, down} per agent. Both stacks consume the **same logical indices**:\\n- PettingZoo: `{agent: int(A[t, i]) for i, agent in enumerate(agents)}` (Discrete(5) action space).\\n- JaxMARL: encode the same index into its native action tensor \\u2014 one-hot 5-vector `jnp.eye(5)[A[t]]` of shape `(n_agents, 5)` (verify JaxMARL SimpleSpread's `action_space`; if it is Discrete(5) use `A[t]` as int32 indices directly). Assert the two encodings carry identical logical actions (one-hot index == Discrete index). Pre-generating offline removes JAX-vs-NumPy RNG cost/stream as a confound vs. on-the-fly seeded sampling (rejected).\\n\\n## 6. Controls & parity assertions\\nControls: (1) identical env config (n_agents=3, n_landmarks=3, L=25, equal obs/action dims asserted); (2) identical hardware + identical core affinity/thread env + identical XLA/system env vars; (3) identical timing methodology (same timer, same loop harness, same warmup concept, resets included identically); (4) common metric formula SPS=N/t; (5) no GPU for either stack (JAX on CPU); (6) same logical action stream; (7) compilation/warmup excluded; (8) same Python minor version.\\n\\n**Runtime parity checks** in `parity_check.py` (run once before benchmarking; both are sanity checks, throughput is the headline claim):\\n- Observation shape per agent = 12 for 3 agents/3 landmarks (own velocity 2 + relative positions of 2 other agents (2\\u00b72) + relative positions of 3 landmarks (2\\u00b73)); assert `obs.shape == (num_agents, 12)` in both stacks and action dim == 5.\\n- Reward trace: on the same 200-step action stream, per-step summed reward must match within tolerance (|r_jax \\u2212 r_pz| < 1e-4 per step or Pearson r \\u2265 0.99), confirming both stacks implement the same Simple Spread objective.\\n\\n## 7. Analysis plan\\n- **Descriptive** (per stack, per metric): mean, median, std, CV, min/max, bootstrap 95% CI (10k percentile resamples).\\n- **Comparative (paired by construction \\u2014 interleaved trials):** Wilcoxon signed-rank on per-trial differences + paired t-test on log-transformed SPS (interpretable as a ratio); median speedup ratio `median(SPS_jax/SPS_pz)` with bootstrap CI (paired resampling); effect sizes: paired Cohen's d (log scale) and Cliff's delta.\\n- **Decision rule applied to Metric A only:** if the bootstrap 95% CI of the ratio excludes 1.0 and p < 0.05, report a significant difference; otherwise report \\\"indicative, inconclusive\\\" (see limitations).\\n- **Robustness:** interleaved order; report %CV; report empty-loop SPS (trivial `pass` loop) as the per-framework-loop overhead floor / measurement ceiling; report B and C as qualitative context with the A/B gap showing per-step overhead share.\\n\\n## 8. Feasibility\\n- **Feasible here?** No \\u2014 the sandbox is Python 3.14.4 with no numpy/jax/gymnasium/pettingzoo and no network (cannot pip install); GPU off, so JAX would be CPU-only in any case. The design targets the Python 3.11 host above (\\u00a73).\\n- **Runtime budget:** on CPU, a jitted JaxMARL single-env step is ~tens of \\u00b5s and a PettingZoo simple_spread step is ~0.1\\u20131 ms; Metric A at N=2000 \\u00d7 R=10 is seconds per stack \\u2014 trivially within budget. Feasible-check sequence at implementation: (1) install + import both; (2) `parity_check.py` passes; (3) smoke run N=100, 1 trial per stack; (4) full N=2000 \\u00d7 R=10; then analyze.\\n- **Dependency risk:** jaxlib wheel availability for the target Python at install time (mitigate: pin within 0.4.x); jaxmarl 0.1.0's dependency surface (chex) \\u2014 only the MPE path is used.\\n\\n## 9. Known limitations\\n1. **CPU-only JAX:** GPU is unavailable (setup_4 says JaxMARL is *GPU-accelerated*, but this measurement is CPU-only); reported JaxMARL numbers are CPU-specific and GPU would change them.\\n2. **Metric C is context only:** PettingZoo has no vectorized path, so C is never pooled into the head-to-head.\\n3. **Indicative power:** N=2000 \\u00d7 R=10 is a lightweight benchmark, not a high-power study; the decision rule (\\u00a77) states this explicitly.\\n4. **Per-step timer overhead:** timing every call in Metric B adds instrumented-loop overhead that can inflate the measured step-only cost of the slower stack \\u2014 hence A is the headline and B is qualitative.\\n5. **Grounding:** repos not present in sandbox; layout/APIs cited from canonical upstream and verified at implementation; small mismatches (e.g., JaxMARL internal jit, action-space encoding) are resolved by the verification step, not assumed away.\\n\\n## 10. Assumptions (my choices, approved) vs fixed requirements\\n**Fixed requirements:** answer the SPS question for MPE Simple Spread under random actions, design-only (no execution claimed), grounded in the repos with a verification step; setup_4/setup_5/setup_9 framing facts.\\n**Assumptions:** Metric A is the headline claim; B and C are context; explicit `jax.jit(env.step)` + AOT warmup is canonical usage; action stream is a pre-generated seeded Discrete(5) index stream encoded natively by each stack; episode length pinned to L=25 by the harness; target host Python 3.11.9 with pinned wheels (numpy 1.26.4, jax/jaxlib 0.4.38 CPU, jaxmarl 0.1.0, pettingzoo 1.24.3, gymnasium 0.29.1, chex 0.1.86); identical core affinity/thread env for both stacks; JAX CPU-only.\", \"evidence\": \"Sandbox inspection: /workspace contains only task.json (explicitly design-only, \\\"repositories and compute are not required\\\"); filesystem-wide searches (find -iname '*jaxmarl*'/*pettingzoo*/*mpe* and deeper) found no relevant source; network disabled (pypi.org DNS resolution failure); none of numpy/scipy/jax/jaxlib/gymnasium/pettingzoo/torch importable under system Python 3.14.4 (x86_64, 224 cores, ~2TB RAM, GPU off); time.perf_counter verified at 1e-9 s resolution with a working no-op probe. Design grounded in canonical upstream layouts: JaxMARL jaxmarl/environments/mpe/simple_spread.py (class SimpleSpread) + common.py (MPEEnv, get_obs/get_reward); PettingZoo pettingzoo/mpe/simple_spread/simple_spread.py (raw_env/parallel_env) with vendored mechanics in pettingzoo/mpe/_mpe_env.py (MPEEnv.action_space Discrete->one-hot translation, World.step dt=0.1, per-agent scenario.observation/reward) \\u2014 all to be verified at implementation time since repos are not present locally.\", \"assumptions\": [\"Metric A (single-env sequential end-to-end loop SPS) is the headline head-to-head claim; Metrics B (per-step-only) and C (JaxMARL vmapped batch) are qualitative context only.\", \"JaxMARL SimpleSpread is run with explicit jax.jit(env.step) + AOT-compile warmup on CPU (GPU off); if the env jits internally the redundant double-jit is documented as harmless.\", \"Random actions = one pre-generated seeded Discrete(5) index stream consumed identically by both stacks, encoded into each env's native action tensor (one-hot 5-vector for JaxMARL if that is its space) with equal logical content asserted.\", \"Episode length pinned to L=25 by the harness for both stacks (equal reset cadence), independent of any internal max_cycles/max_steps semantics.\", \"Execution targets a Python 3.11.9 host with network at install time and pinned wheels: numpy 1.26.4, jax/jaxlib 0.4.38 CPU (patch-level within 0.4.x allowed), jaxmarl 0.1.0, pettingzoo 1.24.3, gymnasium 0.29.1, chex 0.1.86; identical CPU affinity (taskset -c 0-3) and OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 for both stacks.\", \"Both stacks implement the same OpenAI-MPE-derived Simple Spread scenario, so obs shape (12 per agent) and reward semantics match; verified at runtime by parity_check.py with tolerance.\", \"No experiment was executed in this sandbox; the deliverable is a design document.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Exact JaxMARL MPE action representation (Discrete(5) vs continuous 5-vector) and any internal @jit in simple_spread.py/common.py \\u2014 resolved by the implementation-time verification step, not assumed.\", \"jaxlib wheel availability for the target Python at install time \\u2014 mitigated by pinning within jax/jaxlib 0.4.x.\", \"CPU-only JAX dispatch/threading behavior on the 224-core x86_64 host \\u2014 managed via fixed affinity, interleaved trials, and the empty-loop noise floor.\", \"Whether JaxMARL observation/reward traces match PettingZoo exactly vs to tolerance \\u2014 parity is asserted as a sanity check (pass/fail gate) rather than a hard equivalence requirement for the throughput claim.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-b0495d51ec1b4b78b842970bc2b7df50", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Deliver complete experiment design comparing JaxMARL vs PettingZoo MPE Simple Spread SPS", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
